<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaocen ag Mohammed</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaocen ag Mohammed was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> leader of the Kel Ewey from the Aïr in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Linked to the Sanusi order, he led a major revolt against French rule in 1916–1917 and besieged the French garrison of Agadez for several months. After French reinforcements crushed the revolt he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>, where he was captured and hanged in 1919.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued long after the formal conquest: the Bani–Volta war of 1915–1916 in the west of present-day Burkina Faso and Mali, and the Tuareg revolts of 1916–1917 led by <b>Kaocen</b> in Niger and Firhoun in present-day Mali, were among the largest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tuareg resistance culminated in the revolt of <b>Kaocen</b>, who besieged Agadez in 1916-1917.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1916 to 1917 <b>Kaocen ag Mohammed</b>, of a Kel Ewey section, led a major revolt and besieged Agadez, but French forces crushed the uprising in 1917.</blockquote><a href="/state/kel-ewey">Kel Ewey</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1916 and 1917 Sultan Tegama joined the Tuareg revolt of <b>Kaocen</b>, which besieged the French garrison; he was captured and killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-agadez">Sultanate of Agadez</a></li></ul></section>
